commit
git: worktree, Sync the index with a reset target

Resetting the index diffed the target tree against the index as the
worktree sees it, which leaves out the entries flagged SkipWorktree. A
path outside the sparse directories kept the hash it held before the
reset, or stayed in the index after the target dropped it, and the
reset still reported success. Committing afterwards would have restored
the path's earlier content.

Diff the target against every index entry, so the index is brought to
the target for skipped entries too. A rewritten entry keeps its flag,
because the entries are the only record of the sparse state once a
reset does not restate `SparseDirs`. The diff runs over a copy of the
index with the flag cleared, made only when an entry carries it, so
the merkletrie index noder needs no new option.

A skip-worktree entry the reset changes or removes is checked before
anything is written, as Git's `verify_uptodate` checks it when sparse
checkout is off [1]. If its path holds what the entry records, the
reset updates or removes it like any tracked path, and records the
stat data of a file it writes, so Git does not take that file for a
local change. If it holds anything else, every mode that updates the
worktree refuses with `ErrLocalChanges`: hard, keep and merge resets,
and checkouts with or without `Force`. Mixed and soft resets change
only the index, and do not check, as in Git.

Git refuses an entry whose stat data changed. go-git's skip-worktree
entries often carry none, so it compares what the path holds instead:
the file's type, its executable bit when `core.fileMode` is set, and
its contents, as written or, under `core.autocrlf`, with CRLF converted
to LF. `.gitattributes` is not consulted. A gitlink's path may hold
anything when the target keeps a gitlink there or drops the path, as
Git lets a submodule be out of sync with the index; when the target
puts a file or a tree there, the path must be an empty directory,
so the user's files there are not overwritten.

If the path is absent, or lies under a symlinked directory, only the
index changes. Git would write an absent path, but the flag is
go-git's only record of the sparse checkout, so writing it would undo
`SparseDirs` on every later reset or pull. The pass that removes files
outside the sparse directories now removes only those of entries this
reset excludes, so a skip-worktree path the reset does not change is
left alone, as in Git. `ErrLocalChanges` is now documented as returned
by any reset or checkout that would discard local changes.

Tests cover the absent, recorded and modified file against a target
that drops the entry, modifies it or turns it into a directory, in
every mode; a mixed reset; a reset restricted to other files; a
directory or symlink where a file is recorded, a file where a symlink
is, an executable bit the entry lacks, and a path under a file; a
mode-only change; symlink entries; gitlinks kept, dropped, replaced
over user files and over an empty directory; a file turned into a
gitlink; `core.autocrlf`; a path under a symlinked directory; and the
sparse resets that update, remove or keep excluded entries.

[1]: https://github.com/git/git/blob/v2.54.0/unpack-trees.c#L2221-L2277

Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5 (1M context)
Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5
Signed-off-by: Hidde Beydals <hidde@hhh.computer>

